WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.799 --> 00:00:05.250
Hey, David, if there were a mandatory gun
buyback in the United.

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:06.250
Could it really work?

00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:09.020
Would people really participate?

00:00:09.020 --> 00:00:10.470
Very complicated question.

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:13.990
So let's first just make sure we're on the
same page.

00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:19.560
If you mean, could the United States say all
guns must be turned in.

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:22.000
And we will buy them off of you.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:25.720
You don't get that if you don't repeal the
Second Amendment.

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:28.099
I spent some time reading about this.

00:00:28.099 --> 00:00:35.180
I don't see any way that there could be a
nationwide overarching umbrella, mandatory

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:42.390
gun buyback without changing the fact that
people do have a right to own firearms, as

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:43.809
confirmed by the Supreme Court.

00:00:43.809 --> 00:00:48.859
Now, if you want to look at something different,
like, for example, a certain type of firearm

00:00:48.859 --> 00:00:49.859
has been banned.

00:00:49.859 --> 00:00:53.219
And if you have one, you have to turn it in.

00:00:53.219 --> 00:00:58.010
You will have a higher success rate with that,
both legally and in terms of getting getting

00:00:58.010 --> 00:00:59.839
societal participation.

00:00:59.839 --> 00:01:04.770
But I don't see how in a country with a Second
Amendment, you can do a mandatory gun buyback.

00:01:04.770 --> 00:01:08.370
So my first question would be, how do you
address the Second Amendment?

00:01:08.370 --> 00:01:14.410
You have to dismantle that right, which the
Supreme Court precedent supports in order

00:01:14.410 --> 00:01:16.060
to do it.

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:17.400
If you could do it.

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:18.400
OK.

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:19.420
Cost really is an issue.

00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:21.760
I mean, we we've 300 million firearms.

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:27.890
Is it 300 or 350 million firearms floating
around in the United States?

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:29.420
This is why when we talk about.

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:31.810
Well, demilitarize the police.

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:32.810
Yes.

00:01:32.810 --> 00:01:39.280
Police with no guns in the U.S., it's tougher
because so many people have guns in countries

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:40.750
where fewer citizens have guns.

00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:41.750
Sure.

00:01:41.750 --> 00:01:43.670
I think that's an obstacle here.

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:44.670
So.

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:46.130
So then what is the cost?

00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:48.230
Because of the number of guns that are floating
around.

00:01:48.230 --> 00:01:51.860
Could be hundreds of billions of dollars could
could be high, hundreds of billions of dollars,

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:54.050
depending on how it would be structured.

00:01:54.050 --> 00:01:57.380
I know that the government can just you know,
not even it's not even print.

00:01:57.380 --> 00:01:59.730
It's digitally create money.

00:01:59.730 --> 00:02:04.220
But I see that as very tough politically to
imagine.

00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:07.960
And then if you can get through the legal
and the financial.

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:13.670
The truth is that some other much less aggressive
acts haven't been super successful.

00:02:13.670 --> 00:02:21.500
I looked at the New York Safe Act and it saw
of it was based on assault weapons of the

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:28.080
million that are registered in the state,
I believe only 45000 assault weapons were

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:29.080
turned in.

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:30.720
That's very, very low compliance.

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:33.060
Now, there are places that have been able
to do this.

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:40.230
The UK, New Zealand, Australia, they have
smaller populations, fewer guns floating around,

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:41.770
national gun registries.

00:02:41.770 --> 00:02:46.610
So by and large, the country knows where a
lot of those guns are and they don't have

00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:50.000
anything in their constitution that is deemed
gun ownership a right.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:53.340
It's these are laws and the laws can be changed.

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:55.020
They had a lot of success there.

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:59.580
The problem I have believing this would be
feasible as an immediate next step in the

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:02.810
United States, as some have suggested, is
that the genie is out of the bottle in terms

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:07.740
of the Second Amendment, in terms of the number
of guns that are out in circulation.

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:12.020
So I think to really, if this is your priority
and I believe that there is lower hanging

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:15.900
fruit that we should be looking at first to
create a better infrastructure, where then

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:18.340
we could maybe start getting some guns off
of the street.

00:03:18.340 --> 00:03:23.090
But if you want to go first to a gun buyback,
you first have to deal with the Second Amendment.

00:03:23.090 --> 00:03:25.420
You secondly, have to deal with the financial
piece.

00:03:25.420 --> 00:03:28.190
And third, how do you enforce it?

00:03:28.190 --> 00:03:31.630
The law enforcement would not be able to enforce
it.

00:03:31.630 --> 00:03:37.080
And even a few years, given the number of
guns that are out there, what exactly would

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:38.080
be the plan?

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:39.110
I'm not being defeatist.

00:03:39.110 --> 00:03:40.450
I'm just being a realist.

00:03:40.450 --> 00:03:42.750
And I would love for you to tell me where
where I'm wrong on this.

